#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168ff4b3-533f-582d-b25a-979d3efd7f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi^2+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292574} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f81ec-d4a1-5b3b-a720-98fcd4e8ed18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi^2-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86f81ec-d4a1-5b3b-a720-98fcd4e8ed18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\pi^2-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292575} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d323e5-1043-5a99-a402-d3f5e604ca44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c8ed404-9352-554d-b8ea-95293b796d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-352029" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(12x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-12\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-12\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-12(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-12D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-12)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
